1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,175
Could lightning
power the world?

2
00:00:02,175 --> 00:00:04,750
Catch every bolt,
for a whole year.

3
00:00:05,580 --> 00:00:09,045
Lightning is flashing
somewhere right now,

4
00:00:09,045 --> 00:00:12,035
about 44 times
every second,

5
00:00:12,145 --> 00:00:13,695
all around the planet.

6
00:00:13,835 --> 00:00:15,300
That adds up to more

7
00:00:15,300 --> 00:00:17,760
than 1 billion
flashes in a year.

8
00:00:18,180 --> 00:00:19,700
One flash carries

9
00:00:19,700 --> 00:00:21,695
roughly
1 billion joules,

10
00:00:21,825 --> 00:00:24,240
a rough guess
with a wide range.

11
00:00:24,465 --> 00:00:27,520
Multiply it by every
flash in the year.

12
00:00:28,380 --> 00:00:30,865
That is about
400 billion

13
00:00:30,865 --> 00:00:34,275
kilowatt-hours,
a very big number.

14
00:00:34,380 --> 00:00:36,450
But a number only means

15
00:00:36,450 --> 00:00:37,930
something
beside another,

16
00:00:37,940 --> 00:00:40,770
so let's compare
it with what we use.

17
00:00:41,585 --> 00:00:44,360
The world uses
nearly 80 times

18
00:00:44,360 --> 00:00:46,805
more electricity
in a year.

19
00:00:46,805 --> 00:00:48,545
All the lightning
on Earth

20
00:00:48,545 --> 00:00:50,940
would run
it for about five days.

21
00:00:51,780 --> 00:00:54,695
If a flash holds
10 times less

22
00:00:54,695 --> 00:00:57,225
or 10 times more,
it swings

23
00:00:57,225 --> 00:00:59,975
from half a day
to about seven weeks.

24
00:01:00,780 --> 00:01:03,750
And catching
it is the real problem.

25
00:01:03,750 --> 00:01:05,520
The bolts
are everywhere,

26
00:01:05,670 --> 00:01:08,355
and each is over
in a split second.

27
00:01:09,180 --> 00:01:12,765
Next envelope: a what-if
that you ask us next.
